  "summary": "Oro Valley, Arizona's Town Council unanimously authorized $145,887 from a border support grant for its police department to lease four Flock Safety Drone as First Responder units, intended for border-related crimes and general emergency response. The article details the department's existing Flock ALPR use, data-sharing practices with regional partners considered case-by-case, 30-day retention, and responds to DeFlock Tucson's criticism over an audit-log search labeled 'protest.'",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The Oro Valley Town Council authorized the town's police to use $145,887 to lease drones to help detain people charged with drug trafficking, human smuggling, illegal immigration and other border-related crimes",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "In a recent social media post, activist group DeFlock Tucson posted the department's audit logs obtained through a public records request. It showed Oro Valley police logging one search for \"protest.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "Asked if crossing undocumented is a case the department would consider a legitimate reason to access data or footage, Wright said it depends on whether the case was tied to criminal activity.",
      "paragraph_idx": 24
    },
    {
      "quote": "Data from the department's Flock cameras is retained for 30 days.",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    }
  ],
